/*
Problem: Non-overlapping Intervals (LC 435)
Topic: Greedy / Interval Scheduling

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

We want to remove the minimum number
of intervals so that the remaining
intervals do not overlap.

Greedy strategy:

Always keep the interval that finishes
earliest.

Why?

An interval with the earliest ending
time leaves the maximum space for
future intervals.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Sort intervals by ending time.

2. Keep the first interval.

3. Traverse the remaining intervals.

4. If:

   currentEnd > nextStart

   the intervals overlap.

   Remove the current interval by
   counting this interval as removed.

5. Otherwise:

   Keep the new interval by updating
   current.

6. Return the removal count.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[[1,2],[2,3],[3,4],[1,3]]

Sorted by end:

[1,2]

[2,3]

[1,3]

[3,4]

Keep:

[1,2]

↓

[2,3] (no overlap)

↓

[1,3] overlaps

Remove it

↓

[3,4] (no overlap)

Answer:

1

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[[1,2],[1,2],[1,2]]

Keep first interval.

Remove the remaining two.

Answer:

2

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Keeping the interval with the smallest
ending time maximizes the available
space for future intervals.

Any interval ending later cannot
produce a better solution.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n log n)

Sorting dominates.

Traversal is O(n).

Space : O(1)

Ignoring sorting space.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Greedy + Interval Scheduling
*/

class Solution {
public:

    static bool paircmp(vector<int> a,
                        vector<int> b) {

        return a[1] < b[1];
    }

    int eraseOverlapIntervals(
        vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {

        sort(intervals.begin(),
             intervals.end(),
             paircmp);

        vector<int> curr = intervals[0];

        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 1;
             i < intervals.size();
             i++) {

            // Overlap
            if (intervals[i][0] < curr[1]) {

                count++;
            }

            // No overlap
            else {

                curr = intervals[i];
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
};
